  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 224 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSynoptically, central Indiana will be under a slowly progressing \nlongwave pattern with mainly ridging over the eastern half of the \nCONUS along with a broad area of high pressure centered over the \nAppalachian region. Central Indiana will remain on the western half \nof this area of high pressure keeping conditions warmer than normal \nand dry through at least Friday. The combination of high pressure \nnear the surface along with broad subsidence across the region will \nlead to patchy fog along our river basins each morning through \nThursday. \n\nThe mid to upper level pattern will slowly change as we near the end \nof the work week, increasing SW flow and providing a slight warming \ntrend for Thursday and Friday. Current expectation is for highs in \nthe low to mid 60s for each of those days. As the 850-700mb winds \nincrease late this week, diurnal PBL expansion should be able to tap \ninto them leading to occasional wind gusts of 15-20MPH. \n\nConditions will begin to change late Saturday through Sunday as the \nlongwave trough nears central Indiana and pushes surface high \npressure eastward. Shortwaves downstream of this trough will start \npassing through central Indiana as early as Sunday morning, with an \nassociated increase in PoPs. There is some uncertainty on the \nplacement of the longwave trough due to the emergence of warm core \nlows over the western Atlantic basin impeding progression. \nRegardless, the trough should pass through by late Sunday into \nMonday with a modest cold frontal passage. \n\nA return to dry but substantially cooler weather is expected for \nearly next week in the wake of the cold front as another large \nsurface high traverses the area, though this one appears more likely \nto move through quicker than the one anchored over the eastern US \nthis week. Temperatures are likely to be 20 or so degrees colder by \nnext Monday than they were to start the weekend.",
